### What steps can reproduce the bug?

When adding a new header after creating a response with `c.text` (and no status code), Bun sets the `content-type` to `application/octet-stream`.
```typescript
import { Hono } from 'hono'

const app = new Hono()
  .use('/', async (c, next) => {
    await next()
    c.header('test', 'example') // set header after `next` resolves
  })
  .get('/', (c) => {
    return c.text('ok') // text response with no status code
  })

const server = Bun.serve({
  fetch: app.fetch,
})

const response = await fetch(server.url)
console.log(response.headers)

// Headers {
//   "content-type": "application/octet-stream",
//   "content-length": "2",
//   "test": "example",
// }
```
### Why this happens
1. when Hono creates a new `text` response it [uses the `Response` constructor](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/89f4c9691dd688d70e2b707fe87a8b97cb932136/src/context.ts#L682) if no status is set, which assumes that `Response` construction will default `content-type`
2. Bun does _not_ default `content-type` during `Response` construction (unlike Node), so the return of `app.fetch` does not have a `content-type`
3. when modifying headers after `Context` is finalized, Hono's internal `#res` is [reset with the `Response` constructor](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/89f4c9691dd688d70e2b707fe87a8b97cb932136/src/context.ts#L512), i.e., `new Response(#res.body)`
4. after Hono's `app.fetch` resolves, Bun internally defaults the missing `content-type` header. somehow, constructing the `Response` with the body (stream) instead of a `string` in step 3 confuses Bun (see "Additional Information" below)

### What is the expected behavior?

Ideally, the `content-type` would be `text/plain`. I think this could easily be achieved by hard-coding the appropriate header [for new text responses](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/89f4c9691dd688d70e2b707fe87a8b97cb932136/src/context.ts#L683):
```typescript
// ? (new Response(text) as ReturnType<TextRespond>)
? (new Response(text, { headers: { `content-type`: TEXT_PLAIN } }) as ReturnType<TextRespond>)
```
I'm happy to open a PR to make the change, but I also understand that adding code to deal with a Bun-specific issue isn't ideal. It's also sort of an edge-case, since setting a status avoids the issue altogether. It's unexpected behavior though, and it took me a while to figure it out, so I thought I'd share.

### What do you see instead?

_No response_

### Additional information

The following tests demonstrate that:
1. Bun `Response` construction does not set `content-type`: https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/17085
2. The header _is_ set by `Bun.serve`
3. When the `Response` has been constructed using the `body` of another, the resulting `content-type` is `application/octet-stream`

```typescript
import { test, expect } from 'bun:test'

const CONTENT_TYPE = 'content-type'

test('Response constructed with `string` is "text/plain"', async () => {
  const serverResponse = new Response('will be text')
  const serverContentType = serverResponse.headers.get(CONTENT_TYPE)
  expect(serverContentType).toBe(null)

  const server = Bun.serve({
    fetch: () => serverResponse,
  })

  const response = await fetch(server.url)
  await server.stop(true)

  const responseContentType = response.headers.get(CONTENT_TYPE)
  expect(responseContentType).toBe('text/plain;charset=utf-8')
})

test('Response constructed with other `Response.body` is "application/octet-stream"', async () => {
  const rawResponse = new Response('will be octet-stream')
  const serverResponse = new Response(rawResponse.body, rawResponse)
  const serverContentType = serverResponse.headers.get(CONTENT_TYPE)
  expect(serverContentType).toBe(null)

  const server = Bun.serve({
    fetch: () => serverResponse,
  })

  const response = await fetch(server.url)
  await server.stop(true)

  const responseContentType = response.headers.get(CONTENT_TYPE)
